1
00:00:00,083 --> 00:00:04,316
In this video, we're going to
create a custom Braille Embossing Cylinder

2
00:00:04,466 --> 00:00:06,083
from start to finish.

3
00:00:06,083 --> 00:00:09,166
Then download the 3D printable STL file

4
00:00:09,166 --> 00:00:12,383
for both the embossing cylinder
and the counter cylinder.

5
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:15,966
Both of these parts are needed for the 3D
printed Braille Embosser,

6
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:20,000
which impresses
a readable braille dot into cardstock.

7
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:24,150
By the end of this video,
you'll have two printable STL files.

8
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:27,933
In the next section, I'll cover 3D printer
setting recommendations.

9
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:32,050
First, we'll start at the Braille
Customizer app in a web browser.

10
00:00:32,233 --> 00:00:33,700
So here's the starting page.

11
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:36,700
On the right side
you've got your text input area,

12
00:00:37,533 --> 00:00:39,616
language selection

13
00:00:39,616 --> 00:00:41,966
card thickness

14
00:00:41,966 --> 00:00:44,100
and plate selection.

15
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:46,916
Which is where most of the work happens.

16
00:00:46,916 --> 00:00:49,283
On the left is a 3D preview.

17
00:00:49,283 --> 00:00:53,266
It'll update as soon as you press the
generate button at the bottom of the menu.

18
00:00:53,650 --> 00:00:56,383
You can find the accessibility
controls at the top.

19
00:00:56,383 --> 00:00:59,716
You press this button
to switch between Dark mode, Light mode,

20
00:01:00,283 --> 00:01:01,333
and High Contrast mode.

21
00:01:02,433 --> 00:01:03,566
You press this button.

22
00:01:03,566 --> 00:01:06,566
To increase
font size or decrease font size.

23
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:09,483
The 3D preview panel
has its own brightness

24
00:01:09,483 --> 00:01:12,483
and contrast controls.

25
00:01:12,616 --> 00:01:15,666
Before you type anything,
the most important decision

26
00:01:15,666 --> 00:01:18,750
is figuring out
what to put on the embossing cylinder.

27
00:01:19,816 --> 00:01:23,600
For this Braille Embosser,
it allows about 13 Braille characters.

28
00:01:23,933 --> 00:01:24,733
per line.

29
00:01:24,733 --> 00:01:28,216
That means you won't be able to translate
everything from a standard business card.

30
00:01:28,366 --> 00:01:32,250
The Braille Authority of North America
[BANA] has published

31
00:01:32,466 --> 00:01:36,800
a prioritized list of recommendations
For translating a business card

32
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:40,083
into Braille,
for exactly this situation, Translating

33
00:01:40,083 --> 00:01:43,516
your name should be top priority
and then at least one contact method.

34
00:01:43,816 --> 00:01:48,516
Next would then be job title,
then mailing address, then fax number.

35
00:01:48,650 --> 00:01:52,583
For a more detailed explanation,
please click the help button in the top

36
00:01:52,583 --> 00:01:53,266
right corner.

37
00:01:53,266 --> 00:01:55,866
Or use the link in the info panel.

38
00:01:55,866 --> 00:01:59,116
It covers what to include and why,
and a couple of examples,

39
00:01:59,266 --> 00:02:02,266
which is all backed
by [BANA]'s recommendations.

40
00:02:02,416 --> 00:02:05,450
I'll paste all of my business card
information into this Braille

41
00:02:05,450 --> 00:02:07,266
customizer text area.

42
00:02:07,266 --> 00:02:08,016
Right away.

43
00:02:08,016 --> 00:02:11,216
There's a warning telling me
the text exceeds the available space.

44
00:02:11,383 --> 00:02:15,066
That's normal and happens to pretty much
everyone who pastes their full business

45
00:02:15,066 --> 00:02:16,150
card information.

46
00:02:16,150 --> 00:02:19,216
So let's edit it down
based on the [BANA] priority list.

47
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:22,800
My organization is already
in my email address

48
00:02:23,066 --> 00:02:26,000
so I can remove the organization line.

49
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
My job title isn't essential,
so I'll drop that to.

50
00:02:29,383 --> 00:02:32,383
Before moving on to the next step,
here are a few settings worth knowing.

51
00:02:33,066 --> 00:02:36,800
The placement Mode option
is set to auto placement by default.

52
00:02:37,783 --> 00:02:41,033
In this mode, when translating your text
to Braille, The program

53
00:02:41,033 --> 00:02:44,466
automatically handles wrapping
your text across the available lines.

54
00:02:45,050 --> 00:02:48,066
translating capitalized letters
is disabled by default.

55
00:02:48,683 --> 00:02:49,416
In Braille.

56
00:02:49,416 --> 00:02:53,016
To capitalize a letter,
you place capital indicator character

57
00:02:53,083 --> 00:02:55,250
just before the letter
you'd like to capitalize.

58
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:59,433
So each capitalized letter will take two
character spaces instead of just one.

59
00:02:59,733 --> 00:03:03,300
So disabling capitalized
letters will save quite a bit of space.

60
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:07,466
If a person happened to have a name,
that might be confused with a common word,

61
00:03:07,666 --> 00:03:10,566
this could be a good reason
to capitalize the letter.

62
00:03:10,566 --> 00:03:15,050
For example, if somebody happened to have
the name Otter, spelled like the animal,

63
00:03:15,133 --> 00:03:19,300
it could be helpful to capitalize the O
in Otter, to be less confusing.

64
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:24,133
This program uses Liblouis,
In any translation situation,

65
00:03:24,233 --> 00:03:25,650
accuracy is critical.

66
00:03:25,650 --> 00:03:29,583
And that's why I choose to use Liblouis,
which is an open source Braille

67
00:03:29,583 --> 00:03:32,716
translation library
and is widely regarded as one of the most

68
00:03:32,716 --> 00:03:35,716
reputable, freely
available translation tools.

69
00:03:36,233 --> 00:03:39,833
That being said, it's always a good idea
to cross-check your results

70
00:03:39,833 --> 00:03:43,883
with another online Braille translator
program, Or a braille specialist.

71
00:03:44,383 --> 00:03:46,250
Moving on to the next section.

72
00:03:46,250 --> 00:03:48,716
The input language is set to English,

73
00:03:48,716 --> 00:03:52,633
(UEB) uncontracted by default,
which is also a [BANA] recommends.

74
00:03:53,033 --> 00:03:54,316
Thanks to Liblouis,

75
00:03:54,316 --> 00:03:57,766
there are over 50 language
input options available if you need them.

76
00:03:57,916 --> 00:04:00,916
It's important to keep in mind
this isn't translating between

77
00:04:00,983 --> 00:04:04,283
other languages, just between the input
language and Braille.

78
00:04:04,450 --> 00:04:07,850
For example, if you'd like to translate
French into braille,

79
00:04:08,033 --> 00:04:10,616
You'd first need to type
in the information in French

80
00:04:10,616 --> 00:04:13,616
and make sure that the language option
is selected to French.

81
00:04:14,116 --> 00:04:15,250
Okay, next section.

82
00:04:15,250 --> 00:04:18,250
Card thickness controls
the embossing depth.

83
00:04:18,250 --> 00:04:22,766
Right now it's set to 0.4mm,
Which is standard for most business cards.

84
00:04:22,866 --> 00:04:27,233
There is a 0.3 option available
if your card is slightly thinner.

85
00:04:27,466 --> 00:04:29,900
Whichever one you choose,
Please keep a note of it.

86
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:31,450
Both the embossing cylinder

87
00:04:31,450 --> 00:04:35,333
and the counter cylinder must be generated
at the same thickness.

88
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:38,550
Mismatching the setting between
two cylinders, can lead

89
00:04:38,550 --> 00:04:41,766
to the embossing
cylinder’s dots being damaged.

90
00:04:42,333 --> 00:04:45,566
Next step, make sure the embossing plate
option is selected.

91
00:04:45,733 --> 00:04:50,233
In the select plate to generate section,
then go ahead and press the generate

92
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:51,516
STL button.

93
00:04:51,516 --> 00:04:54,900
The generating process can take, anywhere
from a couple of seconds

94
00:04:54,900 --> 00:04:58,266
to a couple of minutes, depending on
what kind of device you are using.

95
00:04:58,533 --> 00:05:02,650
Once it is fully generated, it'll appear
on the left in the 3D preview panel.

96
00:05:02,816 --> 00:05:06,700
If you're using a mouse and keyboard,
You can click on the cylinder to drag,

97
00:05:06,816 --> 00:05:08,716
rotate, scroll and zoom.

98
00:05:08,716 --> 00:05:11,716
Or you can use touch screen controls
do the same thing.

99
00:05:11,733 --> 00:05:13,500
If you've generated your cylinder,

100
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:16,500
you'll be able to see the Braille dots
on the cylinder surface.

101
00:05:16,633 --> 00:05:19,916
Here in my example,
this is the indicator shape area.

102
00:05:20,150 --> 00:05:24,683
Here, you'll see the indicator letters
B, J, and W

103
00:05:25,133 --> 00:05:28,650
which is just the first letter
of each of the first four words.

104
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,166
These indicator letters is to have a built
in labeling system

105
00:05:32,166 --> 00:05:33,516
for non braille readers.

106
00:05:33,516 --> 00:05:33,883
That way

107
00:05:33,883 --> 00:05:35,983
if you print more than one
braille cylinder,

108
00:05:35,983 --> 00:05:38,333
you have a quick way
to identify them apart.

109
00:05:38,333 --> 00:05:41,116
Now let's download the embossing cylinder
we just created.

110
00:05:41,116 --> 00:05:42,816
If it's successfully generated,

111
00:05:42,816 --> 00:05:46,583
Then the Generate STL button
should change to say Download STL.

112
00:05:47,183 --> 00:05:50,183
We'll click it again
and we'll start the download process.

113
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:53,500
The embossing cylinder file
should automatically be named

114
00:05:53,583 --> 00:05:56,583
after the first word
that was typed into the translation.

115
00:05:56,650 --> 00:05:57,883
Before we're done,

116
00:05:57,883 --> 00:06:01,533
we need to generate and download
the counter cylinder to complete the pair.

117
00:06:01,700 --> 00:06:04,633
The counter cylinder
has all the braille dots activated.

118
00:06:04,633 --> 00:06:06,550
It was designed this way,
so that it could be used

119
00:06:06,550 --> 00:06:09,333
with any custom version of Braille
that you might generate.

120
00:06:09,333 --> 00:06:11,933
So as long as the card thickness
setting is the same,

121
00:06:11,933 --> 00:06:14,166
you'll only need to download and print one

122
00:06:14,166 --> 00:06:17,616
counter cylinder for as many custom
cylinders as you would like.

123
00:06:18,116 --> 00:06:19,016
To generate it,

124
00:06:19,016 --> 00:06:23,316
scroll down to the setting plate selection
and select Universal Counterplate.

125
00:06:23,383 --> 00:06:26,083
Then press the Generate STL button again.

126
00:06:26,083 --> 00:06:28,933
Then press the Down STL button again.

127
00:06:28,933 --> 00:06:31,350
If you're going to experiment
with different thickness settings,

128
00:06:31,350 --> 00:06:34,816
I recommend you add that information
to the file name that you download.

129
00:06:34,816 --> 00:06:37,866
For example; counter_cylinder_ 0.4mm.

130
00:06:38,016 --> 00:06:41,016
So that way you don't have to second
guess the size later.

131
00:06:41,066 --> 00:06:45,066
For advanced control, there's an Expert
Mode button at the bottom of the page.

132
00:06:45,383 --> 00:06:49,766
This section lets you control dot shapes,
dot spacing, and many other things.

133
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:51,633
You can

134
00:06:51,633 --> 00:06:54,633
find more information on this section
and help guide.

135
00:06:54,683 --> 00:06:58,466
Now that we've generated and downloaded
both of our STL files,

136
00:06:58,616 --> 00:07:01,600
let's move on to 3D
printer slicer settings.

137
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:02,466
First, import

138
00:07:02,466 --> 00:07:06,716
both STL files into whatever 3D
printing slicer software you'll be using.

139
00:07:06,883 --> 00:07:08,933
I'm going to be using Bambu Lab Studio.

140
00:07:08,933 --> 00:07:11,716
In my experience,
the seam painting feature

141
00:07:11,716 --> 00:07:14,716
makes a significant difference in Braille
dot quality.

142
00:07:14,883 --> 00:07:17,550
Most automatic scene placement settings

143
00:07:17,550 --> 00:07:21,066
will try to hide the seam,
in a visually inconspicuous spot.

144
00:07:21,233 --> 00:07:25,233
Commonplaces are corners, edges,
or inside recesses.

145
00:07:25,383 --> 00:07:28,750
It's totally fine for normal prints,
but it becomes a big problem

146
00:07:28,850 --> 00:07:30,816
in our Braille embossing process.

147
00:07:30,816 --> 00:07:34,616
If the seam is placed either
on a braille dot or within the recess

148
00:07:34,616 --> 00:07:38,566
of a braille dot, it can significantly
change the embossing dimensions.

149
00:07:38,650 --> 00:07:41,566
So I recommend using the seam
painting tool.

150
00:07:41,566 --> 00:07:45,150
So that way you can control the seams
and place them away from all of the dots.

151
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:49,083
I've set the seam painting brush
to vertical mode and increase

152
00:07:49,083 --> 00:07:50,250
the size to four.

153
00:07:50,250 --> 00:07:53,066
I've chosen to paint over
the indicator letters and triangles.

154
00:07:53,066 --> 00:07:56,166
This now tells the slicer
to put the seams in this area only.

155
00:07:56,250 --> 00:07:58,450
Don't forget to also do the counter
cylinder.

156
00:07:58,450 --> 00:08:02,050
I'm using a Bamboo Lab X1C 3D printer,
and the following

157
00:08:02,050 --> 00:08:04,116
settings of what worked best for me.

158
00:08:04,116 --> 00:08:07,016
I start with Bamboo
Lab Studio's high quality preset,

159
00:08:07,016 --> 00:08:10,733
but I've still had pretty good results
for anything smaller than 0.2mm.

160
00:08:10,883 --> 00:08:13,766
Here are a few changes
I made from the default setting.

161
00:08:13,766 --> 00:08:18,250
First, I've changed the wall
generator from Classic to Arachne.

162
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:21,433
Arachne handles
the small details of the Braille dots

163
00:08:21,433 --> 00:08:22,883
a little more consistently,

164
00:08:22,883 --> 00:08:24,416
The biggest difference I've noticed

165
00:08:24,416 --> 00:08:26,933
is a slightly more accurate recessed
braille dot.

166
00:08:26,933 --> 00:08:27,616
Next.

167
00:08:27,616 --> 00:08:30,400
I reduce all default speeds to about half.

168
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:33,833
So initial layer to 40, infill to 75,

169
00:08:34,083 --> 00:08:37,016
outer wall to 50, inner wall to 75,

170
00:08:37,016 --> 00:08:40,783
small perimeters to 40, and halved
all the accelerations as well.

171
00:08:40,950 --> 00:08:44,233
For my printer, I didn't need any supports
and I didn't need a brim.

172
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:46,133
These are just my starting points,

173
00:08:46,133 --> 00:08:48,800
so if you're still getting clean prints
at a higher speed.

174
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:50,500
You should just keep your settings

175
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:52,866
But if you're seeing Braille
dot equality issues.

176
00:08:52,866 --> 00:08:55,716
I'd recommend slowing down the 3D
print as a starting point.

177
00:08:55,716 --> 00:09:00,266
With these settings,
using basic PLA on a bamboo lab X1C

178
00:09:00,483 --> 00:09:03,333
I can 3D print
both cylinders in about three hours.

179
00:09:03,333 --> 00:09:06,800
This braille embosser
was designed to maximize efficiency.

180
00:09:06,966 --> 00:09:11,766
Once the outer housing mechanism is 3D
printed and a counter cylinder is printed,

181
00:09:11,950 --> 00:09:15,483
it's much easier to update information
or add information,

182
00:09:15,783 --> 00:09:19,850
since you'll only need to 3D
print a new custom embossing cylinder

183
00:09:19,850 --> 00:09:23,083
which only takes about an hour
and a half on my type of machine.

184
00:09:23,250 --> 00:09:27,333
In a group environment,
like an office or a classroom, Each person

185
00:09:27,333 --> 00:09:31,000
would only need the 3D print their
individual custom embossing cylinder,

186
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,716
which only takes an hour and a half,
and a small amount of 3D filament.

187
00:09:36,266 --> 00:09:36,800
And that's it!
